WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 119: James Monroe and the Cumberland Road: Paving the Path to the American West

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.920
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our exploration of President James Monroe by looking inward at a nation transforming its own wilderness.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:20.480
[Rebecca] In the 1820s, America was swept by the era of good feelings, a rare moment of political peace.

5
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:28.560
[Rebecca] But between the established coastal states and the expanding frontier stood a wall of stone and dense timber, the Appalachian Mountains.

6
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:35.920
[Rebecca] Moving goods across the mountains took weeks of grueling toil and cost more than shipping cargo across the Atlantic Ocean.

7
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:43.200
[Rebecca] To bind the continent together, the young nation embarked on its first great public works project, the National Road.

8
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:51.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Cumberland Road and the pioneering spirit that conquered the mountains.

9
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:11.920
[George] The Appalachian mountain range ran like a colossal, jagged spine for 1,500 miles from Maine to Georgia, separating the settled eastern seaboard from the fertile, virgin lands of the Ohio Valley and the Northwest Territory.

10
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:19.040
[George] For pioneer families seeking a new life beyond the mountains, the journey was an agonizing, perilous ordeal.

11
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.120
[George] There were no paved roads.

12
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:33.680
[George] Travelers were forced to follow ancient Native American hunting paths and old military routes hacked out decades earlier by George Washington and British General Edward Braddock during the French and Indian War.

13
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:42.800
[George] In spring, these dirt trails dissolved into bottomless quagmires of liquid mud where wagons sank to their axles and horses drowned in potholes.

14
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.040
[George] In summer, they baked into rock hard ruts that shattered wagon wheels and snapped wooden axle trees.

15
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.680
[George] In winter, freezing blizzards and ice-coated mountain passes trapped travelers in high-altitude wilderness.

16
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.560
[George] The economic consequences were devastating.

17
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:09.320
[George] It cost more than 100 to haul a single ton of freight from Philadelphia to Pittsburgh, far more than the cost of shipping that same ton across the Atlantic Ocean to Europe.

18
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:21.640
[George] Farmers in Ohio and Kentucky could not afford to ship their grain, whiskey, and livestock east over the mountains, and were forced to float their goods down the Mississippi River to Spanish-controlled New Orleans.

19
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:35.400
[George] Visionary leaders like George Washington and Albert Gallatin had long warned that unless commercial arteries were built across the mountains, the Western settlers would inevitably form their own separate country, dissolving the American Union.

20
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.320
[George] The dream of a national highway began to take concrete shape during Thomas Jefferson's presidency.

21
00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:52.840
[George] In 1802, when Ohio was admitted to the Union as the 17th state, Congress passed an enabling act containing a visionary provision devised by Treasury Secretary Albert Gallatin.

22
00:02:52.840 --> 00:03:05.880
[George] The law decreed that 5% of the net proceeds from the sale of federal public lands in Ohio would be set aside to survey and construct a great national road connecting the waters of the Atlantic Ocean to the Ohio River.

23
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:12.520
[George] In 186, Jefferson signed the historic legislation authorizing the construction of the Cumberland Road.

24
00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:19.880
[George] The route was carefully surveyed, it would begin at Cumberland, Maryland, at the head of navigation on the Potomac River.

25
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:33.480
[George] Cross the formidable Allegheny Mountains through Pennsylvania, pass through the panhandle of Virginia at Wheeling on the Ohio River, and eventually push westward through Ohio, Indiana, and Illinois toward the Mississippi River.

26
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:40.840
[George] Construction officially began in 1811, but was soon brought to a near-total standstill by the War of 1812.

27
00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.840
[George] The war itself provided a brutal lesson in the vital necessity of national infrastructure.

28
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:56.960
[George] The American military had suffered catastrophic delays attempting to move cannons, ammunition, and troops to Lake Erie and the Canadian frontier over impassable wilderness tracks.

29
00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:06.800
[George] By the time the war ended, both the public and political leaders recognized that national roads were not a luxury, but a vital matter of national survival.

30
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:17.040
[George] When James Monroe took the oath of office as president in March 1817, the United States was entering a golden age of national pride and political harmony.

31
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.280
[George] The Federalist Party, having opposed the War of 1812, had collapsed into political irrelevance.

32
00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.680
[George] Monroe's Democratic Republicans held virtually unchallenged authority.

33
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:36.320
[George] To consolidate this spirit of national reconciliation, the 59-year-old Monroe embarked on an extraordinary, unprecedented journey.

34
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:50.800
[George] Dressed in the buff and blue uniform of a revolutionary war officer, with his powdered hair tied in a cue and a tricorn hat upon his head, Monroe set out on a 15-week, 3,000-mile tour of New England and the northern frontier.

35
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.320
[George] He was the first president to visit Boston since George Washington nearly thirty years earlier.

36
00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:04.560
[George] New Englanders, who had fiercely opposed Monroe's party for decades, turned out by the tens of thousands to cheer the revolutionary veteran.

37
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:11.120
[George] Triumphal arches were erected, church bells pealed, and festive banquets were held in every town.

38
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:21.920
[George] When Monroe visited Boston, a leading Federalist newspaper, the Colombian Sentinel, marveled at the warmth of his reception and coined a phrase that permanently defined the era.

39
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.840
[George] The era of good feelings.

40
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.840
[George] Throughout his tour, Monroe inspected naval yards, forts, canals, and roadworks.

41
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:37.560
[George] He saw firsthand how modern infrastructure could knit disparate regions together into a unified, prosperous continental republic.

42
00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:44.840
[George] Monroe returned to Washington determined to throw the full weight of the federal government behind the completion of the Cumberland Road.

43
00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:55.400
[George] Carving a 66-foot-wide highway through the rugged virgin forests and granite crags of the Allegheny Mountains was an engineering triumph that captured the imagination of the world.

44
00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:07.720
[George] Thousands of laborers, many of them newly arrived Irish and German immigrants alongside local frontiersmen, tackled the wild slopes with axes, pickaxes, shovels, and black blasting powder.

45
00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:11.240
[George] The specifications laid down by Congress were demanding.

46
00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:19.160
[George] The right of way had to be cleared to a width of 66 feet, with a central roadway twenty feet wide covered with crushed stone.

47
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:30.360
[George] To prevent the road from washing away in torrential mountain rains, American engineers adopted a revolutionary method newly pioneered in Scotland by engineer John Laudon MacAdam.

48
00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:39.720
[George] Known as the McAdam process, the roadbed was meticulously designed with a raised, crowned center so water would drain into roadside ditches.

49
00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:54.120
[George] Workers laid a foundation of heavy twelve-inch stones, followed by successive layers of smaller rocks, broken by hand with sledgehammers until every fragment was small enough to fit inside a laborer's mouth or pass through a three-inch ring.

50
00:06:54.120 --> 00:07:05.640
[George] When heavy iron wagon wheels rolled over these crushed stone layers, the sharp edges interlocked, creating a dense, smooth, hard wearing surface that resisted frost and rain.

51
00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:17.360
[George] Spanning the roaring mountain torrents were dozens of magnificent, hand-hewn stone arch bridges, constructed with dry masonry keystones that have stood impervious to floods for more than two centuries.

52
00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:26.640
[George] By 1818, the Cumberland Road had successfully breached the crest of the Alleghenies, reaching the Ohio River at Wheeling, Virginia.

53
00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:35.200
[George] Almost overnight, the national pike became the most bustling, colorful and economically vital highway on the North American continent.

54
00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.280
[George] The highway was crowded day and night with a relentless tide of traffic.

55
00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:54.240
[George] The monarchs of the road were the massive Conestoga wagons, known as the ships of the inland seas, built with curved, boat-shaped beds to keep cargo from shifting on steep mountain grades, and covered with towering white canvas sheets.

56
00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.200
[George] These lumbering giants were hauled by teams of six powerful draft horses adorned with chiming brass bells.

57
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:13.040
[George] A single Conestoga wagon could haul up to 10,000 pounds of freight, cutting shipping costs between Baltimore and Wheeling by more than half and slashing travel time from a month to less than a week.

58
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:21.360
[George] Alongside the freight wagons dashed the glamorous stagecoaches, painted in brilliant shades of yellow, red, and royal blue.

59
00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:35.920
[George] Carrying the United States male and wealthy passengers, the coach drivers blew brass bugles to announce their arrival, driving their four-horse teams at a breakneck 10 miles per hour and changing horses at way stations every twelve miles.

60
00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:41.680
[George] Every mile of the road featured bustling taverns, blacksmith shops and drover yards.

61
00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.960
[George] Taverns were divided strictly by social class.

62
00:08:44.960 --> 00:09:02.760
[George] Elegant stage houses served fine roasts and Madeira wine to stagecoach travelers, while rough wagon stands catered to teamsters, offering hearty bacon, corn pone, and whiskey for three cents a cup, with drivers sleeping wrapped in blankets around roaring common room fireplaces.

63
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:09.720
[George] The Cumberland Road was not merely a highway, it was the vibrant, beating heart of early American commerce.

64
00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:19.400
[George] As traffic exploded on the Cumberland Road, the surface suffered immense wear and tear from thousands of iron-rimmed wheels and the pounding hooves of cattle herds.

65
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:27.320
[George] The road was in desperate need of constant maintenance, but Congress faced a perplexing question: who was going to pay for it.

66
00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:39.880
[George] In 1822, Congress passed a bill authorizing the federal government to erect toll gates on the Cumberland Road, collect tolls from travelers, and impose federal fines on anyone evading payment.

67
00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:46.760
[George] The bill landed on President James Monroe's desk, presenting him with a profound constitutional crisis.

68
00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:57.240
[George] Like his mentors Jefferson and Madison, Monroe was a strict constructionist who believed the federal government possessed only those powers explicitly enumerated in the Constitution.

69
00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:03.240
[George] In a dramatic move, Monroe vetoed the Cumberland Road Bill on May 4, 1822.

70
00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:09.720
[George] Munro accompanied his veto with a masterly, 40-page treatise on the nature of American federalism.

71
00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:24.120
[George] He explained that while he passionately supported internal improvements, the Constitution nowhere gave Congress the jurisdiction to operate toll gates, enforce criminal penalties, or establish sovereignty over land within individual states.

72
00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.880
[George] To operate toll gates, Monroe argued, would require a constitutional amendment.

73
00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:33.400
[George] However, Monroe offered an ingenious compromise.

74
00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:44.120
[George] While Congress could not exercise local jurisdiction, it did have the constitutional power under the General Welfare Clause to appropriate federal money for projects of national importance.

75
00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:59.440
[George] Two years later, Monroe signed the Landmark General Survey Act of 1824, allowing the Army Corps of Engineers to survey vital national roads and canals, establishing a permanent federal partnership in American infrastructure development.

76
00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.280
[George] Monroe's constitutional compromise resolved the crisis.

77
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:20.240
[George] The federal government repaired the roadbed, and in the 1830s, surrendered ownership and maintenance of individual sections to the states of Maryland, Pennsylvania, and Virginia, which erected stone toll houses and collected toll revenues to maintain the highway in perpetuity.

78
00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:24.960
[George] Meanwhile, the National Road continued its unstoppable march westward.

79
00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:39.760
[George] Crossing the Ohio River, the road pushed through Zanesville, Columbus, and Springfield in Ohio, cut through the heart of Indiana, and finally reached Vandalia, Illinois, spanning more than 600 miles across the American continent.

80
00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:47.600
[George] Over this stone corridor poured hundreds of thousands of settlers who transformed the Midwest into the agricultural breadbasket of the world.

81
00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:55.600
[George] Though the rise of steam railroads in the 1850s eventually eclipsed horse-drawn wagon traffic, the National Road never died.

82
00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:03.360
[George] In the 20th century, with the dawn of the automobile age, the ancient Macadam route of the Cumberland Road became the path for U.S.

83
00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.960
[George] Route 40, the main street of America, and later the corridor for Interstate 70.

84
00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:17.040
[George] James Monroe understood that a great republic could not survive if its citizens remained isolated strangers divided by mountain walls.

85
00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:31.880
[George] By helping carve the Cumberland Road through the wilderness, Monroe and the founding generation built the physical spine of the American Republic, proving that free people could not only declare liberty on parchment, but pave its path across a continent.

86
00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:36.440
[George] The Cumberland Road was more than crushed stone and arched bridges.

87
00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:42.520
[Rebecca] It was the physical connection that bound East and West into a single, indivisible nation.

88
00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:48.280
[Rebecca] James Monroe's stewardship ensured that the frontier became the engine of American growth.

89
00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:55.560
[Rebecca] Next week, we turned to America's sixth president, John Quincy Adams, and his extraordinary second act.

90
00:12:55.560 --> 00:13:06.600
[Rebecca] Returning to the House of Representatives as an elder statesman to wage a lonely, heroic eight year crusade against the notorious gag rule to defend the right of petition against slavery.

91
00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:11.160
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

